Negative control for the frame-reserved column widths (E7).

Reserves the share for ONE declared column and measures every other one from
whatever is in it, so the widths are the current page's data rather than the
frame's reservation. That is E7's defect — the columns resize and jump as a sort
swaps the rows under them.

The drop has to be PARTIAL to be a control: content sizing applied to all of
them leaves the header row uniform again, and uniformity across the declared
columns is the whole of what the E7 spec can see (jsdom resolves no widths, so
"equal share" is asserted as one shared treatment, never a measured pixel).

Re-pointed for `R7-2`: the header's width is a variant now, not one fixed class,
because an icon column measures to its content by its RENDERER's own answer.
Only E7's half is withdrawn here — the star column keeps its intrinsic width, so
`R7-2`'s own case stays green and this control still tries exactly one law.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the actions-anchor spec's E7 block — the declared columns no longer share
one width treatment, so they are measured from their content.

